<section class="panel" aria-label="Grade 3 Unit 4 Lesson 6 Division as an Unknown Factor"><h2>Multiplication and division as related equations</h2><p>3 × □ = 12 and 12 ÷ 3 = □: both blanks are <strong>4</strong>.</p><h2>Original onion story: two ways to name the unknown</h2><p>Fourteen onions distributed equally into two bags. Lin: <strong>2 × □ = 14</strong>. Mai: <strong>14 ÷ 2 = □</strong>. Both missing quantities are seven onions per bag.</p><div role="img" aria-label="Two groups, each containing seven first-party onion counters" style="display:flex;gap:.6rem"><span style="border:1px solid #687d88;border-radius:.3rem;padding:.4rem">●●●●●●●</span><span style="border:1px solid #687d88;border-radius:.3rem;padding:.4rem">●●●●●●●</span></div><p><strong>Source figure gate:</strong> original activity “At the Farmers’ Market” has a multirow diagram/table, but the actual rows are not available in the local text. No table-row coverage is claimed. Review each row with the source figure or original student task PDF and teacher before advancing.</p><p>The publisher Boxed Muffins Cool-down is gated, and the source photo is not reproduced.</p><p><a href="https://im.kendallhunt.com/k5/teachers/grade-3/unit-4/lesson-6/lesson.html" rel="noopener noreferrer">Original 2021 lesson</a></p></section>
